A||||3||dgroves||Tach reading wrong||||1||
Z||000000||dgroves||08-26-2001||03:33 PM||||The tach in my 86GT is acting screwy. At rest the thing works fine. I have checked it with a remote tach that connectd to the #1 plug wire. The problem is when I drive the car, the readings seem to double. The tach hand will go all the way around to the stop.(about 3 o'clock)I'm sure that it's not turning THAT fast. Would a faulty tach filter cause this kind of behavior? Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||dgroves||08-29-2001||02:40 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||08-29-2001||03:45 PM||||I had this very problem in my 86. what I found is the resistor on the back of the Tach circut board, HAD a piece of tape on it to protect the graphite resistor. For some reason I think water had gotten on it and somehow changed it's value. <P>The FIX<BR>The easiest way to fix this is replace the tach.<BR>OR<BR>I took a number 2 pencil and scribbled on the resistor until it was coated then put it all back together. I started the engine and reached my finger up into the tach and rubbed off some of the graphite until it read the right RPM. I did this over 3 years ago and it still works even to this day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-29-2001||07:27 PM||||There's a thread in the last couple days about recalibrating the tach for a V8. There's a link in it that will explain the resistor. You want the adjustable version of the modification.||||reg||1
